From ba32a8a4ba20accf66b58031da3414b069305a56 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michiel <367462+meghuizen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 10:27:44 +0200
Subject: [PATCH nf-next 1/3] netfilter: conntrack: hash IPv4 tuples as two
 words

hash_conntrack_raw() runs siphash() over the first 39 bytes of the
tuple on every conntrack lookup, i.e. once per packet, and again at
confirm and in the NAT unique-tuple search.  For IPv4, 24 of those
bytes are the two nf_inet_addr unions, of which 4 bytes each are used.
siphash() consumes 39 bytes as four 8-byte blocks plus a tail: 14
SipHash rounds.

Pack the IPv4 addresses, ports, l3num and protonum into two u64 and use
siphash_2u64(): 10 rounds, no loop, no tail handling.  The key is
unchanged.  The value is only used as a bucket index and lookups still
compare the full tuple.  IPv6 keeps the generic path.

Instructions retired per call of the lib/siphash.o functions built from
this tree, linked into a userspace loop and counted with perf stat
(20M calls, loop overhead of 11 instructions subtracted on x86_64):

                     siphash(), 39 bytes   siphash_2u64()
  x86_64                     256                164
  i386 (not subtracted)      986                627

Packing the words adds 12 instructions to hash_conntrack_raw() on
x86_64 against 2 for the generic call, and about 24 against 3 on i386
(objdump of nf_conntrack_core.o).  Net per IPv4 hash: about -80
instructions on x86_64 and -330 on i386.  IPv6 gains a compare and a
not-taken branch.  Wall-clock was not measured.

In the built kernel (x86_64, same config) the IPv4 path of
hash_conntrack_raw() calls siphash_2u64() (165 instructions, no loop)
instead of __siphash_unaligned(); hash_conntrack_raw() grows from 54 to
70 instructions for the packing.

Runtime, x86_64 guest (nested KVM, 16 vCPUs), kernel instructions per
packet forwarded through a netns router with conntrack (perf stat
instructions:k), patches 1-3 against the base commit, booted
interleaved: 2 boots with the series, 7 base boots:

                         base, median (range)     patches 1-3
  new flow, per packet   16845 (16835-17180)      16684, 16688
  established stream     14422 (14330-14884)      14299, 14322

The new-flow change, about -155, is two IPv4 hashes; patch 2 only runs
at deletion and patch 3 is not on the packet path.  The expected -80
per established-stream packet is inside the per-boot spread.

Tested: nf_conntrack_core.o builds without warnings (W=1) on x86_64 and
i386 at this commit; the runtime run above.

Assisted-by: Claude perf
---
Impact: medium -- IPv4 conntrack lookup (once per packet through
conntrack) and flow setup, on routers and hosts with conntrack loaded.
Instruction counts only (static, userspace and in-kernel);
no wall-clock measurement.

- The harness (available on request) links the siphash functions
  compiled from this tree into a userspace loop, so the counted code is
  the kernel's own.
- Static counts from a userspace copy of the primitives (GCC 15.2 -O2,
  straight-line, not measured on hardware): Cortex-A53 177 -> 128,
  rv64gc 411 -> 297, rv64gc_zbb 245 -> 179.
- siphash() is the 64-bit SipHash on every architecture (only hsiphash()
  switches to HalfSipHash on 32-bit), which is why i386 gains most.
- IPv6 pays one compare and branch more than before.

 net/netfilter/nf_conntrack_core.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/net/netfilter/nf_conntrack_core.c b/net/netfilter/nf_conntrack_core.c
index d0d9e5ea84a0..24b3f2b58227 100644
--- a/net/netfilter/nf_conntrack_core.c
+++ b/net/netfilter/nf_conntrack_core.c
@@ -220,6 +220,18 @@ static u32 hash_conntrack_raw(const struct nf_conntrack_tuple *tuple,
 	key.key[0] ^= zoneid;
 	key.key[1] ^= net_hash_mix(net);
 
+	/* An IPv4 tuple fits in two words.  Hashing those takes 10 SipHash
+	 * rounds; the generic 39-byte form below takes 14.
+	 */
+	if (tuple->src.l3num == AF_INET)
+		return siphash_2u64((u64)tuple->src.u3.ip << 32 |
+					    tuple->dst.u3.ip,
+				    (u64)tuple->src.u.all << 48 |
+					    (u64)tuple->dst.u.all << 32 |
+					    (u64)tuple->src.l3num << 8 |
+					    tuple->dst.protonum,
+				    &key);
+
 	return siphash((void *)tuple,
 			offsetofend(struct nf_conntrack_tuple, dst.__nfct_hash_offsetend),
 			&key);
